##OOD

There are a lot of controversial statements in here, that the main stream programming/OOP/OOD community will strongly disagree with.  I believe this is mainly because 1) main stream community misunderstands OOD, 2) Hiding in our favourite languages are major flaws (4. below) that the community does not acknowledge.

Robert Martin and OOD in a nutshell:
1. In structured programming both the run time and compile time dependency flow is top down.  Main calls high level functions, which call .... etc. etc.  High level code depends on the lowest level details.
2. The top down compile time dependency is *precicely* backwards from the what we want for robust, anti-fragile code.  If we can find a way to turn this around, then we can decouple our code modules.
3. What is OOP? a) Encapsulation, b) Inheritance, c) polymorphism
  Encapsulation: C had perfect encupsulation. In a header file you could forward declare data structures and functions that manipulate those data structure.  C++ (and all of its decentents java, C# etc) broke this by forcing us to declare data members in the class interface.   We conclude modern language support for encapsulation is medicore at best.  So we soldier on.
  Inheritance: If we want to take advantage of polymorphism most modern OO languages, we are forced to use inheritance.  So that's what we do.  There are languages the support polymorphism without inheritance. 
  Polymorphism: This is the key to solving the The top down compile time dependency problem outlined in 1. and 2.  What we need from OOP is polymorphism.  Inheritance and Ecapsulation (private: protected:) are mostly side issues.  Polymorphism also allows us to restore encapsulation.  Many authors also  include (adding confusion) templates and function/operator overloading under the name Polymorpism by adding the "static" qualifier.  While static polymorphism is also useful for clean code it does preciesly nothing to help solve the dependency flow problem outlined in 2.
4. C++ supports polymorphism but it has flaws: private: protected: are hacks. Polymorphism should not be coupled to inheritance.  Same for java and C# and they additionally disallow multiple inheritance which is a major design flaw in these languages.
5. We can invert the compile time dependency problem in 2. using SOLID::DIP, the dependency inversion principle.  High level functions depend on abstract (C++ pure virtual) interfaces.  The low level details are hiding in the classes that implement these interfaces.  These classes are hidden from the high level functions. Yes, somewhere (main or a factory function) we need to construct instances of those classes.  But that build code is very contained and isolated.  Perhaps 1% of LOC!  99% of our LOC see only the interfaces.  Massive decoupling .... iff done correctly.

Architecture is about intent, not about the tools.

